#	the kernels

Three kernels are built from this directory.  Each has one directory and
names nothing outside it; what is shared is the host toolchain in
../utils, the flavour-independent headers in ../include, and libraries
such as libsec, libmp, libfis and libkern.

inferno		the native Inferno kernel
9front		the 9front kernel, as vendored
osprey		Osprey: 9front's kernel with the Osprey policy layer in it

#	what is inside one

Each is laid out the way Plan 9 and Inferno lay out a kernel: the
portable code in port, one directory for each machine beside it, and the
rest as siblings.  A machine directory reads the portable code as
../port.

port		the portable kernel
pc		PC drivers, shared by that flavour's PC machines
pc64		the amd64 PC machine
ip		the Internet protocol stack, Ether and PPP media
boot		bootstrap programs; see the README there
init		the Limbo program the native kernel runs at startup

Osprey has three more of its own: policy, the portable Osprey layer;
adapter, the one file where that layer and the kernel meet; and cmd, the
user programs that drive it.

Headers that belong to a target rather than to a source tree are in the
target: Inferno/amd64/include, 9front/amd64/include, Osprey/include and
Osprey/amd64/include.

